3 Consequences of water pollution in the ocean
Oil spills almost always have an impact on local wildlife or aquatic life, but the potential for spread is enormous.
The increase in oil spills and maritime spills has caused marine pollution. Important: The oil is insoluble in water and will form a thick layer of oil in the water, suffocating fish and blocking light from photosynthetic aquatic plants.
Surface water includes natural water found on the Earth's surface, such as rivers, lakes, ponds, and oceans. These substances come into contact with water and dissolve or physically mix with it.
In developing countries, people drink untreated water directly from rivers, streams, or other sources. sometimes occurs a natural contamination caused by microorganisms such as viruses, bacteria and protozoa.
Many wastewater, fertilizers, and sewage contain high amounts of nutrients that can promote algae and weed growth in the water, make it undrinkable, and even clog filters.
Precisely, this international organization defines water contamination as the contamination of water whose composition changes until it becomes unusable. Contaminated water means that humans cannot use this precious resource.
Of all the types of water pollution, the main ones are related to industrial activity after World War II and to this day. For example, each year more than 450 cubic kilometers of wastewater are dumped into the sea.
Some liquids can contaminate large areas of water in low concentrations. For example, just 4 liters of gasoline can contaminate up to 2,8 million liters of water.
The coasts of France, Spain and Italy are some of the most polluted regions on Earth. Next on the list are the Caribbean, Celtic, and North Seas.
If we don't love our planet and take action to eliminate water pollution, the oceans could pass from our allies in mitigating the effects of climate change to our enemies. These large bodies of water act as natural sinks for carbon dioxide in the atmosphere.
Furthermore, water scarcity and hydric stress are other problems that we have to face. According to estimates by the United Nations Environment Program, by 2025, half of the planet's inhabitants will face a shortage of this precious resource.
Avoiding water contamination is in our hands. These are some of the things we can do to eliminate the presence of contaminants in our water:
Eliminate the use of pesticides and other types of chemicals that threaten our nature
Do not irrigate crops with contaminated water
